== 5. The Fewness and Poverty of the Godly

#strong[The fifth device that Satan has to draw souls away from religious services, and to keep souls from holy duties and heavenly performances, is,]

By presenting to them the fewness and poverty of those who walk in the ways of God, who hold on in religious practices. Satan says, “Do you#super[+] not see that those who walk in such and such religious ways are the poorest, the#footnote[John 7:47, 48, 49.] lowliest and the most despised persons in the world?” This worked on them in John 7: “Then the Pharisees replied, ‘Have you#super[+] also been deceived? Have any of the rulers or Pharisees believed in Him? But this crowd that does not know the law, they are under a curse.’ ”

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device#footnote[“Do you#super[+] not see,” says Chrysostom, “that the places where treasures are hidden are rough and overgrown with thorns? Do not the naturalists tell you#super[+] that the mountains that are big with gold within are bare of grass without?” Saints, like scholars, have poor rations here, because they must strive hard to go to heaven.] of Satan is to consider that though they are outwardly poor, yet they are inwardly rich; though they are poor in temporal things, yet they are rich in spiritual things. The worth and riches of the saints are inward: “The king’s daughter is all glorious within.” “Listen, my beloved brothers: Has not God chosen the poor of this world to be rich in faith and to inherit the kingdom He promised those who love Him?” says James. “I know your poverty, though you are rich!” says John to the church of Smyrna. What if they have little in possession? Yet they have a glorious kingdom in store for them. “Do not be afraid, little flock, for your#super[+] Father is pleased to give you#super[+] the kingdom.” Though saints have little in hand, yet they have much in hope. You#super[+] count those happy, in a worldly sense, who have much coming to them, though they have only a little in possession. And will you#super[+] count the saints miserable because they have little in hand, little in possession, though they have a glorious kingdom coming to them after this? I am sure the poorest saint who breathes would not exchange (were it in his power) what he has in hope and in store for the possession of as many worlds as there are stars in heaven or sands in the sea, etc.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that in all ages God has had some who were great, rich, wise and honorable, who have chosen his ways and cleaved to his service in the face of all difficulties.#footnote[“Good nobles,” says one, “are like black swans, thinly scattered in the firmament of a state, like stars of the first magnitude.” Yet God has had some in all ages, as might be shown from the histories.] Though not many wise men have, yet some wise men have; though not many mighty, yet some mighty have; and though not many noble, yet some noble have. Witness Abraham, Jacob, Job, and various kings and others that the Scriptures speak of. And ah, how many we have among ourselves whose souls have cleaved to the Lord, who have swum to his service through the blood of the slain, and who have not counted their lives dear to them, so that they and others might enjoy the holy things of Christ according to the mind and heart of Christ, etc.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that the spiritual riches of the poorest saints infinitely surpass the temporal riches of all the wicked men in the#footnote[Alexander’s vast mind asked whether there were any more worlds to conquer.] world. Their spiritual riches satisfy them; they can sit down satisfied with the riches of grace that are in Christ, without honors and without riches, etc. “Whoever drinks the water I give him will never thirst.” The riches of poor saints are lasting; they will share bed and board with them; they#footnote[Crassus was so rich that he maintained an army from his own revenues; yet he, his great army, and his son and heir fell together, and left his great estate to others.] will go with them to prison, to a sickbed, to the grave, indeed to heaven. The spiritual riches of poor saints are as wine to cheer them, as bread to strengthen them, as clothes to warm them, and as armor to protect them. Now all of you#super[+] who know anything know that the riches of this world cannot satisfy the souls of men, and that they are as fading as a flower, or as their owners are, etc.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device is to consider seriously that though the saints, considered comparatively, are few; though they are “a little, little flock,”#footnote[Luke 12:32.] a remnant, “a garden locked up, a spring enclosed,”#footnote[Song 4:12.] “a fountain sealed”; though they are like the summer gleanings, one from a city and two from a tribe; though they are#footnote[Jer 3:14.] only a handful to a houseful, a spark to a flame, a drop to the ocean; yet consider them simply in themselves, and they are an innumerable number that cannot be counted, as John says: “After this I looked and saw a multitude too large to count, from every nation#footnote[Rev 7:9.] and tribe and people and tongue, standing before the throne and before the Lamb. They were wearing white robes and holding palm branches in their hands.” So Matthew says: “I say#footnote[Matt 8:11. Heb 12:22, 23. When Fulgentius saw the nobility of Rome sitting high in their splendor, it lifted his meditation up to the heavenly Jerusalem.] to you#super[+] that many will come from the east and the west to share the banquet with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven.” So Paul: “But you#super[+] have come to Mount Zion, to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem. You#super[+] have come to an innumerable company of angels in joyful assembly, to the congregation of the firstborn, enrolled in heaven. You#super[+] have come to God the Judge of all, to the spirits of the righteous made perfect.”

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that it will be but as a day before these poor despised saints shine brighter than the sun in its glory. It will not be long before you#super[+] will wish, “Oh, that we were now among the poor, lowly, despised ones, in the day that God comes to gather up his jewels!” It will not be long before these poor, few saints are lifted up on their thrones to judge the multitude, the world, as the apostle says: “Do you#super[+] not know that the saints will judge the world?” And in that day, oh, how the great and the rich, the#footnote[Mr. Foxe, once being asked whether he knew a certain poor man who had received help from him in a time of trouble, answered, “I remember him well. I tell you, I forget lords and ladies to remember such.” So will the Lord deal with his poor saints: he will forget the great and mighty ones of the world to remember his few, poor, despised ones. Though John was poor in the world, yet the Holy Spirit calls him the greatest born of women. Ah, poor saints! However low the thoughts that men who do not know your#super[+] worth may have of you#super[+], the Lord will have high ones.] learned and the noble will wish that they had lived and spent their days with these few, poor, despised people in the service of the Lord! Oh, how this wicked world will curse the day that they ever had such base thoughts of the poor, lowly saints, and that their poverty became a stumbling block to keep them from the ways of holiness! I have read of Ingo, an ancient king of the Draves, who, making a stately feast, appointed his nobles, at that time pagans, to sit in the hall below, and commanded certain poor Christians to be brought up into his presence chamber, to sit with him at his table and eat and drink of his kingly fare. When many wondered at this, he said that he counted Christians, however poor, a greater ornament at his table, and more worthy of his company, than the greatest peers unconverted to the Christian faith; for while these might be thrust down to hell, those might be his companions and fellow princes in heaven. You#super[+] know how to apply it. Although you#super[+] sometimes see the stars by their reflection in a puddle, or in the bottom of a well, indeed, in a stinking ditch, yet the stars have their place in heaven. So, though you#super[+] see a godly man in a poor, miserable, low, despised condition as to the things of this world, yet he is fixed in heaven, in the region of heaven. “He raised us up,” says the apostle, “and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms in Christ Jesus.” Oh, therefore, say to your#super[+] own souls (when they begin to turn from the ways of Zion because of the poverty and fewness of those who walk in them), “The day is at hand when these few, poor, despised saints will shine in glory, when they will judge this world, and when all the wicked of the world will wish that they were in their condition, and would give ten thousand worlds (were it in their power) just to have the honor and happiness of waiting on those whom they have neglected and despised in this world for their poverty and fewness.”

The sixth and last #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that there will come a time, even in this#footnote[The following Scriptures abundantly confirm this truth: Jer 31:12. Isa 30:23. Isa 62:8, 9. Joel 2:23, 24. Mic 4:6. Amos 9:13, 14. Zech 8:12. Isa 41:18, 19. Isa 55:13. Isa 66:6, 7. Isa 65:21, 22. Isa 61:4. Isa 60:10. Ezek 36:10.] life, in this world, when the reproach and contempt now cast on the ways of God, because of the poverty and fewness of those who walk in them, will be quite taken away. God will do it by making those the head who have been the tail for days without number, and by raising to much outward riches, prosperity and glory those who have been like outcasts because of their poverty and fewness. John, speaking of the glory of the church, the new Jerusalem that came down from heaven, in Revelation 21, tells us: “By its light the nations of those who are saved will walk, and into it the kings of the earth will bring their glory.” So the prophet Isaiah: “They will bring their sons from afar, and their silver and gold with#footnote[Only take these two cautions. First, that in these times the saints’ chief comforts, delights and contentments will consist in their more clear, full and constant enjoyment of God. 2. That they will have such an abundant measure of the Spirit poured out on them that their riches and outward glory will not be snares to them, but golden steps to lead them to a richer living in God.] them,” v. 9. “Instead of bronze I will bring gold; I will bring silver in place of iron, bronze instead of wood, and iron instead of stones,” v. 17. And so the prophet Zechariah speaks, Zech 14:14: “And the wealth of all the surrounding nations will be collected: gold, silver, and apparel in great abundance.” The Lord has promised “that the meek will inherit the earth,” and that heaven and earth will pass away before one jot or the smallest stroke of his word goes unfulfilled. Ah, poor saints! Now some push hard against you#super[+], others look askance at you#super[+], others shut the door against you#super[+], others turn their backs on you#super[+]\; and most men (except a few who live much in God and are filled with the riches of Christ) either neglect you#super[+] or despise you#super[+] because of your#super[+] poverty. But the day is coming when you#super[+] will be lifted up above the dunghill, when you#super[+] will exchange poverty for riches, your#super[+] rags for robes, your#super[+] reproach for a crown of honor, your#super[+] infamy for glory, even in this world.

And this is not all: God will also mightily increase the number of his chosen ones; multitudes will be converted to him. “Who has heard#footnote[Isa 66:8.] of such as this? Who has seen such things? Can a country be born in a day or a nation be delivered in an instant? Yet as soon as Zion was in labor, she gave birth to her children. And they will bring all your#super[+] brothers from all the nations as a gift to the #smallcaps[Lord] on#footnote[Verses 19, 20.] horses and chariots and wagons, on mules and camels, to My holy mountain Jerusalem,” says the #smallcaps[Lord], “just as the Israelites bring an offering in a clean vessel to the house of the #smallcaps[Lord].” Does not the Scripture say “that the kingdoms of#footnote[Rev 11:15.] this world must become the kingdoms of our Lord”? Has not God given Christ “the nations” and “the ends of the earth” for his possession? Has not the Lord said “that in the last days the mountain of the house of the #smallcaps[Lord]#footnote[Ps 2:8.] will be established as the chief of the mountains; it will be raised above the hills, and all nations will stream to it”? Please read#footnote[Isa 2:2. Isa 54:14 and Isa 61:9.] and meditate on the sixtieth chapter of Isaiah, and the sixty-sixth chapter, and the first five verses of the second chapter of Isaiah, and there you#super[+] will find the multitudes that will be converted to Christ. And oh, that you#super[+] would be mighty in believing and in wrestling with God, that he would hasten the day of his glory, so that the reproach now on his people and ways may cease!
